  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday Night through Saturday)\nIssued at 1140 PM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nAny lingering storms should begin to wane after sunset Sunday.\nStorms developing across the High Plains Sunday afternoon could\nmove east and affect parts of northeast Oklahoma late Sunday night\ninto Monday morning while weakening. A good gradient in\ntemperatures is expected on Monday, with relatively cooler weather\nwith some clouds north of the boundary, possibly stopping the 100\ndegree streak for Tulsa and surrounding areas, while temperatures \nremain near and in excess of 100 degrees to the south. Widely \nscattered storm development is again forecast along the boundary \nMonday afternoon, with higher chances shifting near and south of \nI-40 through Monday evening. Again, strong downburst winds will \nbe possible with the strongest storms. Meaningful rainfall will \nremain spotty at best with many locations not seeing much in the \nway of rainfall totals through Monday night. \n\nThe boundary loses definition by Tuesday as the upper ridge \nintensifies over the Southern Plains and a return to very hot \ntemperatures and heat headlines areawide. Guidance continues to\nindicate the ridge shifting westward by mid week with another\nfront approaching the region by late in the day Wednesday. \nAhead of this boundary, Wednesday could be the hottest day of the \nyear as a very strong low level thermal ridge sets up over \nnortheast Oklahoma while southwesterly downslope flow enhances \nwarming through the region. High temperatures of 105 plus degrees \ncould be common Wednesday afternoon across eastern Oklahoma.\n\nThe passing front brings at least some slightly cooler\ntemperatures and more rain chances to parts of the area, though\nthe forecast still remains fairly hot and mostly dry through the\nperiod. A few days on the fringes of northwesterly flow aloft will\nmaintain some low rain chances into the weekend, but widespread\nmeaningful rainfall still appears sparse and drought will likely \npersist or intensify over much of eastern Oklahoma and western \nArkansas. The ridge looks to shift back over the Southern Plains \nby late next weekend maintaining hot and dry conditions.\n\nBowlan",
